---
Attacks attempted, per the dispatch instructions:

1. REPRODUCIBILITY — regenerated with the pinned recipe from tailwind.config.js's
   comment (`npx --yes tailwindcss@3.4.17 -c tailwind.config.js -i
   web/static/css/tailwind.src.css -o /tmp/t7/regen.css --minify`, node22,
   into scratch, not overwriting the repo). `cmp /tmp/t7/regen.css
   web/static/css/tailwind.css` -> byte-identical (59176 bytes both). No repo
   package.json/package-lock/node_modules leftovers (`git status --short`
   clean of any). PASS.

2. ATTACKED THE ORACLE (swarm/t7-coverage.sh) — found a real gap, then
   checked whether it currently causes a defect:
   - The perl extractor only walks `web/templates/**/*.html` and
     `web/static/js/**/*.js`. It never reads any `internal/` or `cmd/` Go
     file. Its "Go code" coverage is entirely the hand-typed
     `@dynamic_safelist` array, cross-checked only against itself (does the
     literal safelist string show up in the CSS) — it is not derived from
     Go source by extraction.
   - I independently grepped `internal/` and `cmd/` for `class="` outside
     `_test.go` files and found THREE Go handler files the oracle never
     looks at that embed literal Tailwind classes in HTML fragments:
     `internal/handlers/whatif/handlers.go:617-624`,
     `internal/handlers/majorexpenses/handlers.go:609-616`,
     `internal/handlers/accounts/handlers.go:669-676` — each a
     `renderError()` building `<div class="p-4 bg-red-50
     dark:bg-red-900/30 border border-red-200 dark:border-red-800
     rounded-lg">...<span class="text-red-700 dark:text-red-300
     font-medium">Error</span>...<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-red-600
     dark:text-red-400">%s</p></div>` via `fmt.Sprintf`. None of these
     tokens are in the `dynamic_safelist`/`safelist`, and the manifest's
     stronger claim ("every class the app can ever emit ... is present in
     the built CSS") is not actually proven by the oracle for this code path
     — CHANGELOG/tailwind.config.js's own scoping is honest here (they only
     claim render.go, not "all Go code"), so this isn't a misrepresentation,
     but it is a real fragility: a future edit to these three functions
     could silently ship unstyled error banners and the gate would still
     say exit 0.
   - I then checked whether the CURRENT tokens from those three files are
     actually present in the built CSS (correcting for Tailwind's
     backslash-escaped selectors, e.g. `.dark\:bg-red-900\/30:is(.dark *)`):
     every one of `p-4, bg-red-50, dark:bg-red-900/30, border,
     border-red-200, dark:border-red-800, rounded-lg, flex, items-center,
     w-5, h-5, text-red-500, dark:text-red-400, mr-2, mt-2, text-sm,
     text-red-600, text-red-700, dark:text-red-300, font-medium` resolves to
     a real selector in `web/static/css/tailwind.css` (confirmed via
     python re-check with correct CSS escaping, not the buggy naive grep I
     tried first). They are covered today, coincidentally, because the same
     literal tokens also occur inside scanned `.html` templates (e.g.
     `accounts_card.html`, `guardrails.html`). No live counterexample found.
   - Also found `internal/handlers/approval/handlers.go` embeds its own
     literal `class="tool"/"detail"/"primary"/"danger"/"note"` HTML, but it
     ships a fully self-contained inline `<style>pageCSS</style>` (its own
     hand-written CSS, not Tailwind, not the CDN) — out of scope for this
     Tailwind-vendoring task, not a regression.

3. VARIANT/ARBITRARY-VALUE STACKS — sampled hostile cases from templates:
   `text-[10px]`/`text-[11px]` (kpis.html, insights/major-expenses/whatif
   verdict bars), `min-w-[14rem]`/`min-w-[12rem]`, `max-h-[80vh]`/`max-h-[50vh]`
   (kpi-detail.html), the full toggle-switch `after:` stack in
   spending-phases.html (`after:content-['']`, `after:top-[2px]`,
   `after:left-[2px]`, `after:h-5`, `after:w-5`, `peer-checked:after:*`,
   `peer-focus:ring-4`, `dark:peer-focus:ring-blue-800`), `top-1/2`,
   `-translate-y-1/2` — all present as real selectors in the built CSS once
   checked with correct CSS-identifier escaping (backslash before `[`, `]`,
   `/`, `:`; `:is(.dark *)` wrapper for `dark:` variants). PASS.

4. UNLOCK PAGE — `git diff web/templates/pages/unlock.html` shows the same
   CDN-script-to-`<link href="/static/css/tailwind.css">` swap as base.html,
   nothing else in its `<head>` references a remote host. Started the real
   server (`BUDGET_DATA_DIR=/tmp/t7/data go run ./cmd/server`, no encryption
   configured) and fetched live: `/static/css/tailwind.css` -> 200,
   `text/css`, 59176 bytes (matches committed file exactly);
   `/` -> redirects to `/dashboard` (200) whose rendered HTML contains the
   `<link>` tag and zero occurrences of `cdn.tailwindcss` or `cdn.`; hitting
   `/unlock` directly redirected away since no encryption is configured in
   this bare setup (matches the task's documented expectation) — verified
   statically instead, confirmed correct. `web/embed.go` has
   `//go:embed templates static`, so `tailwind.css` is embedded for
   production (non-debug) builds too, not just filesystem-served in dev.

5. OFFLINE SWEEP — `grep -rEn "https?://" web/templates/ web/static/js/
   web/static/css/styles.css` (styles.css and js/templates only, excluding
   the built tailwind.css whose only http:// hits are inert
   `xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"` data-URI/namespace strings) returns
   nothing outside `w3.org` SVG namespace strings in JS
   (`document.createElementNS`) — no new remote styling dependency.

6. REGRESSION — `go build ./...` exit 0. `go test -count=1
   ./internal/handlers/...` -> all 10 packages ok (accounts, approval,
   backup, dashboard, duplicates, explorer, insights, majorexpenses,
   transfers, whatif). `git diff --stat web/static/css/styles.css` empty
   (untouched). `git status --short` shows no `.go` files touched, matching
   the CHANGELOG's own claim ("No Go code changed").

`bash swarm/t7-coverage.sh` re-run: exit 0, "OK: 715 literal utility-looking
class tokens covered (45 whitelisted, 50 dynamic-safelist entries verified
present)" — matches the manifest claim exactly.

No concrete counterexample (an emittable class absent from the built CSS)
was found despite deliberately probing the oracle's known blind spot (Go
handler files outside render.go), variant/arbitrary-value stacks, the
unlock page, and reproducibility. The one real weakness found — the oracle
provides no independent extraction/verification for Tailwind classes
embedded in `internal/handlers/{whatif,majorexpenses,accounts}/handlers.go`
`renderError()` — is a legitimate gap in the proof's rigor (a future edit
there is unguarded) but does not make the task's claim false today; it is
reported as a finding, not grounds for FAIL.
